  "narrative": "In the 2020s, Black and African women's hairstyles and hair culture continue to thrive, building upon the rich traditions of their ancestors. The natural hair movement remains a powerful force, encouraging individuals to celebrate their afro-textured locks in all their glory. Styles like the afro, cornrows, box braids, and locs are staples of Black beauty, each with its own unique history and cultural significance. These hairstyles not only reflect individual identity but also serve as a connection to one's heritage. Protective styles, such as Bantu knots, remain essential for preserving hair health in the face of environmental stressors. While documentation from this era is limited, it is clear that Black and African women continue to push boundaries and redefine beauty standards, embracing their natural textures with pride.",
